  "live_records": {
    "q-41031": {
      "dom_id": "step-q41031",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{a}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=3,$ $c=1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{a}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
