    "q-140524": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140524",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-2}{1}=-2.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
