    "q-81212": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81212",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 3}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=6,$ $b=\\frac{9}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(6)}{(\\frac{9}{2})}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
