    "q-86850": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86850",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{b}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{0\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+(-5)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-3)}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=-\\frac{13}{3},$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{-1}{(-\\frac{13}{3})}=\\frac{3}{13}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
